.TH NVIM-TMUX-OPEN 1 "2026-06-30" "termnav" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nvim-tmux-open \- route terminal open requests into Neovim
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nvim-tmux-open
.B cli
.I file
.RI [ cwd ]
.br
.B nvim-tmux-open
.B link
.I target
.RI [ context ...]
.br
.B nvim-tmux-open
.B tmux-link
.I target
.RI [ context ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nvim-tmux-open
opens local or remote terminal targets in an existing Neovim session. The mode
is explicit so normal
.B nvim
command-line arguments stay separate from terminal link text, tmux click text,
and remote-routing metadata.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B cli
Open a literal file target in the current-window Neovim context. The optional
.I cwd
is used to resolve relative file names. CLI mode intentionally does not parse
normal Neovim flags, multiple files, or
.I file:line
syntax; terminal-link normalization is kept in
.B link
and
.B tmux-link
modes.
.TP
.B link
Interpret terminal or WezTerm link text and route it to the local or remote
Neovim context when possible.
.TP
.B tmux-link
Interpret a tmux-originated link or click target.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The target was routed successfully. In
.B tmux-link
mode, an unroutable target is also translated into a tmux-facing message and
reported as success so background tmux bindings do not expose shell failures to
the terminal.
.TP
.B 1
The requested open operation failed in strict
.B cli
or
.B link
mode.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux-follow-click (1),
.BR nvim-link-host (1),
.BR nvim-ssh-control-open (1)
